CTM STACK builds one card shell in Lua. cas: sympy is the provisional default; cas: maxima stops the build until its backend is installed. A mixed-backend page is noted once at build time. Each backend generates its own variants: SymPy seeds use Python’s RNG and do not reproduce Moodle’s deployed seeds.
The version-2 JSON island contains common input/qtest/checker data, backend.question and per-variant plan data that the controller treats as opaque, display HTML, and {n, id, hash} identities. stackRevision reports the engine’s actual reference pin; SymPy’s runtimeHash is null.
H1 sets common.checker to {source, value} for a block checker; otherwise it is null. The SymPy backend keeps the same source and weight in its opaque question plan. Checker plans carry the selected {n, id, hash} identity. The text-only stack.check continuation accepts validated raw/canonical inputs, exact teacher and variable text, assumptions and optional node values. For an XML node, node.sans names its validated input, node.tans is the evaluated teacher text, and node.options is the complete XML testoptions object (checker and optional options). The shared entry resolves these for both block checkers and library predicates; author code receives decoded node values and the inner literal options object. Library matrix assessments are keyed by the same input name. It returns the usual STACK envelope, including author errors that retain the session. SymPy prints canonical input from the written AST until the shared JS admission exists. See the checker contract.
Each backend descriptor is {id, version, adapter, requires: {isolation}}. The adapter URL is relative to the component script. Its script registers a factory under window.CTMStackAdapters[id], returning:

start(): a promise that resolves when requests may run;
request({type, question, variant, answers}): a promise of {ok, result} or {ok: false, error: {kind, code, message}};
stop(reason): stop this backend only; resolves with {restarted};
restart(reason): start a new session for this backend;
isolationRequired: whether this backend requires cross-origin isolation.

The controller has no backend-specific branch. It serializes requests per backend, drops queued requests superseded by the same card/kind, and ignores stale results after edits. The calculation clock starts after start(); loading has its own bound. Stop keeps answers, interrupts first when the shared runtime can do so, and otherwise restarts only the private backend. Runtime errors offer Retry; author errors preserve the session. Invalid answers and undecidable CAS tests remain ordinary results.
SymPy starts CTMWorker with publish: false, then loads SymPy and the STACK checker part from the page. It never assigns or stops mainPyodide. Its adapter normalizes ctm-checkers’ additive errorDetail envelope. MathJax and matrix input are shared page assets. Error and message arguments are data; only backend-sanitized CASText HTML is inserted as HTML.
stack: settings include cas, lang, feedback, stop-after, save, source-panels, and validation. The intended validation default is instant; until the backend’s bounded admission is shipped, SymPy uses the explicit Validate button. API 2 is sufficient; CTMStore is probed at runtime. No-AI builds have no feedback payload, button or gear. Exam delivery retains the umbrella’s Pyodide-only restrictions.

Choose the mathematical test
Use the XML’s ordinary answer tests for equivalence, written form and numeric tolerance. For matrix properties, PythonChecker nodes can select these predicates from ctm_checkers.matrix:








Name
Options
What the node checks




basis
space: nullspace, columnspace or rowspace
A basis for the space of the matrix in &lt;tans&gt;


matrix_property
property: integer_entries, determinant or identity
The indicated property equals &lt;tans&gt;


is_hermitian
None
Hermitian symmetry equals the boolean &lt;tans&gt;


is_positive_definite
None
Positivity of the Hermitian part equals &lt;tans&gt;


is_hpd
None
Hermitian symmetry and positive definiteness together



For example, a basis node uses:
&lt;answertest&gt;PythonChecker&lt;/answertest&gt;
&lt;sans&gt;ans&lt;/sans&gt;
&lt;tans&gt;A&lt;/tans&gt;
&lt;testoptions&gt;{"checker":"basis","options":{"space":"nullspace"}}&lt;/testoptions&gt;
A is the defining matrix, not a displayed basis. The basis predicate accepts any independent family that spans the required space. It reports green, red and dependent-vector assessments without preferring an ordering. Library predicates keep their exact rational-domain and matrix-size restrictions. A question using them needs no checker filename or block checker:.

Symmetric positive definite matrices
For a real matrix, is_hpd requires symmetry and positive definiteness. For a complex matrix, it requires Hermitian symmetry and positive definiteness. The library checks every leading principal minor; a positive determinant alone is insufficient. Use integers or exact fractions for real and imaginary parts and %i for the imaginary unit.
